  "headline": "Corn down 6 straight days to $4.59½; beans down 7 to $12.45",
  "lead": "Corn closed Tuesday at $4.59½, its sixth lower close in a row, and soybeans at $12.45 made it seven. A corn bushel is worth about 15 cents less than a week ago. Harvest is pushing the selling, and nothing new came overnight.",
  "teaser": "Corn and beans keep sliding on harvest selling while crude holds near $100 and cattle push higher.",
  "one_number": {
    "value": "$20",
    "unit": "per barrel to ship oil",
    "context": "Goldman Sachs says the average freight rate to move oil has gained to $20 a barrel from $3 last year, because of the Hormuz shipping crisis."
  },
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Grains keep sliding on harvest",
      "body": "- Dec corn is down 2½ cents overnight, and Nov soybeans are down 3¼ cents.\n- Wheat is down 4¾ cents to $6.38½.\n- AgWeb blames harvest and technical selling.",
      "so_what": "Harvest supply is the story; nothing new hit the market overnight.",
      "conviction_level": "low",
      "overnight_surprise": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Cattle and hogs firm; crude near $100",
      "body": "- Live cattle settled Tuesday at **$229.45**, up 0.9%, feeders gained 0.4% to $345.90, and hogs added 1.2% to $67.15.\n- AgWeb says cattle made near-term highs, and the Columbus, N.M., port resumes cattle trade Oct. 26.\n- WTI is at $100.02 overnight, down 1.7%, and the Energy Department is pressing refiners to raise diesel output.",
      "so_what": "Cattle are firm, but diesel is the cost still pinching harvest.",
      "conviction_level": "medium",
      "overnight_surprise": false
    }
